From: Llaminar runtime qualification
Subject: [PATCH] Publish consumed completions of empty captured graph segments

An empty-only segment captures no AQL packets. BuildSyncPlan nevertheless
allocates a completion signal when another stream consumes that segment.
Previously no packet decremented the signal, permanently blocking its consumer.
Use one ordered completion barrier when there is no packet to carry the signal;
segments with no consumer still submit no unnecessary packet. Packet capture,
parallel queues and the native signal-pool lifecycle remain unchanged.

Pinned base: ROCm/rocm-systems 6b0e43f341195e203754e08f850e437ff2fc09f9
             (therock-10.0, ROCm Core SDK 10.0.0)

diff --git a/hipamd/src/hip_graph_internal.cpp b/hipamd/src/hip_graph_internal.cpp
--- a/hipamd/src/hip_graph_internal.cpp
+++ b/hipamd/src/hip_graph_internal.cpp
@@ -400,6 +400,14 @@ void Graph::ResolveSegmentDependencies() {
 }
 
 // ================================================================================================
+/**
+ * @brief Bind the captured segment DAG to its required completion publications.
+ *
+ * A segment may contain only empty dependency nodes and therefore capture no
+ * packets. If another stream consumes its completion, it still needs a packet
+ * that publishes that signal; reserving a signal without a producer deadlocks
+ * the consumer. Segments with no observable completion need no such packet.
+ */
 void GraphExecSegmented::BuildSyncPlan() {
   // Clean up any prior barrier packets
   for (auto* p : sync_plan_.barrier_packets) { delete[] p; }
@@ -583,7 +591,12 @@ void GraphExecSegmented::BuildSyncPlan() {
     const bool completion_signal_needed = (hw_slot >= 0);
 
     auto& lastBatch = segBatch.packet_batches.back();
-    if (last_node_uncaptured && completion_signal_needed) {
+    if ((last_node_uncaptured || lastBatch.dispatchPackets.empty()) &&
+        completion_signal_needed) {
+      // Empty-only segments have no dispatch on which to embed the completion.
+      // Publish it with the same ordered barrier used for uncaptured tails.
+      // This also imports any incoming launch-stream work before a fork; it is
+      // a device-side dependency, not a host wait or a serial graph replay.
       uint8_t* completion_barrier = device->CreateBarrierPacket();
       sync_plan_.barrier_packets.push_back(completion_barrier);
 
